A text editor keeps history as `{ past, present, future }`, and `set` pushes the old `present` onto `past` and clears `future`. Several undos followed by redos must step back and forth in the right order. Which `undo` and `redo` pair is correct?

```js
// Version 1
case 'undo': return { past: past.slice(0, -1), present: past.at(-1), future: [present, ...future] };
case 'redo': return { past: [...past, present], present: future[0], future: future.slice(1) };

// Version 2
case 'undo': { const prev = past.pop(); return { past, present: prev, future: [present, ...future] }; }
case 'redo': { const next = future.shift(); return { past: [...past, present], present: next, future }; }

// Version 3
case 'undo': return { past: past.slice(0, -1), present: past.at(-1), future: [present, ...future] };
case 'redo': return { past: [...past, present], present: future.at(-1), future: future.slice(0, -1) };

// Version 4 (state is { previous, present })
case 'undo': return { previous: present, present: previous };
case 'redo': return { previous: present, present: previous };
```
(Assume each case returns `state` unchanged when the needed stack is empty.)
